1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,635
A release script backports three fixes in
one command: it cherry-picks them, copying

2
00:00:09,675 --> 00:00:14,255
each fix onto the release branch as a
new commit. It runs in CI, the team's

3
00:00:14,295 --> 00:00:17,951
automation, at two in the morning.
The second fix conflicts.

4
00:00:17,991 --> 00:00:22,263
The script doesn't check the exit
status. It goes on to tag and build.

5
00:00:22,603 --> 00:00:27,150
In the morning the release branch has one
of the three fixes on it, a conflict in

6
00:00:27,190 --> 00:00:31,341
the index, Git's staging file, and a
tag on a commit that nobody intended.

7
00:00:31,381 --> 00:00:35,759
An engineer opens the workspace and has
to answer one question before touching

8
00:00:35,799 --> 00:00:40,459
anything: where exactly did this stop, and
what has already happened to the branch?

9
00:00:40,799 --> 00:00:44,984
The answer is different from the one you
learned for a stopped rebase, and the

10
00:00:45,024 --> 00:00:49,535
difference is what makes this state dangerous.
Keep the engineer's question in mind.

11
00:00:49,575 --> 00:00:50,943
You'll answer it yourself.

12
00:00:52,783 --> 00:00:56,540
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:56,580 --> 00:01:00,087
In the last video every
cherry-pick finished in one step.

14
00:01:00,127 --> 00:01:01,083
Today they stop.

15
00:01:01,423 --> 00:01:06,495
A cherry-pick can stop for two reasons. A
conflict, where Git can't decide a file's

16
00:01:06,535 --> 00:01:11,669
content and waits for you. Or an empty
result, because the change is already on your

17
00:01:11,709 --> 00:01:12,100
branch.

18
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:17,266
You'll look at four things. The state of
a stopped pick: one root ref, and for a

19
00:01:17,306 --> 00:01:22,193
range, a sequencer directory. The four
exits, and where the branch is after each.

20
00:01:22,233 --> 00:01:26,938
A conflict in detail, including a base
that shows lines your branch never had.

21
00:01:26,978 --> 00:01:28,337
And the --empty option.

22
00:01:28,677 --> 00:01:32,095
Throughout, keep the stopped
rebase from video 53 in mind.

23
00:01:32,135 --> 00:01:36,864
The comparison between the two is today's
interview question, and one fact in it

24
00:01:36,904 --> 00:01:41,752
surprises people: during a stopped range
pick, HEAD, Git's name for where you are,

25
00:01:41,792 --> 00:01:44,792
is on the branch, and the
branch has already moved.

26
00:01:46,632 --> 00:01:48,210
Here's what you'll walk away with.

27
00:01:48,550 --> 00:01:49,963
After this video you can:

28
00:01:50,303 --> 00:01:53,993
Read the state of a stopped
cherry-pick from git status and .git.

29
00:01:54,333 --> 00:02:00,912
Choose between --continue, --skip, --abort
and --quit, and say where the branch is

30
00:02:00,952 --> 00:02:01,800
after each.

31
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:05,472
Explain where stage 1 of a
cherry-pick conflict comes from.

32
00:02:05,812 --> 00:02:08,645
Handle a pick that became
empty with --empty.

33
00:02:08,985 --> 00:02:11,675
Compare a stopped range
pick with a stopped rebase.

34
00:02:13,515 --> 00:02:18,686
The state. In one sentence: a stopped
cherry-pick is described by one root ref,

35
00:02:18,726 --> 00:02:23,831
CHERRY_PICK_HEAD, and, when more than
one commit was requested, by a directory

36
00:02:23,871 --> 00:02:29,108
.git/sequencer/ that holds the rest of
the plan. A root ref is a name in capital

37
00:02:29,148 --> 00:02:32,406
letters that lives directly
in the dot git folder.

38
00:02:32,746 --> 00:02:36,266
CHERRY_PICK_HEAD is the commit
that could not be applied.

39
00:02:36,306 --> 00:02:41,451
On screen it sits on ebcee16, on main. In
.git/sequencer/, the file todo holds that

40
00:02:41,491 --> 00:02:46,510
commit and everything after it, and head
is where HEAD was before the first pick.

41
00:02:46,550 --> 00:02:50,945
A cherry-pick of a single commit
creates no sequencer directory at all.

42
00:02:50,985 --> 00:02:54,817
CHERRY_PICK_HEAD and the prepared
message are its whole state.

43
00:02:55,157 --> 00:02:59,160
Now the difference from a
rebase. HEAD is not detached.

44
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:03,276
The picks that have already
succeeded are on the branch.

45
00:03:03,316 --> 00:03:09,083
The branch has moved. Look at release/1.4:
one copy, e0c8403, is already on it.

46
00:03:09,423 --> 00:03:14,279
Think about why. A rebase builds a replacement
history off to the side and switches

47
00:03:14,319 --> 00:03:19,234
the branch over at the end. A cherry-pick
adds commits to the branch you are on, one

48
00:03:19,274 --> 00:03:21,358
at a time. There is no "at the end".

49
00:03:21,698 --> 00:03:22,649
The four exits.

50
00:03:22,989 --> 00:03:27,400
The textbook labels --skip,
--abort and --quit 🟡 CAUTION.

51
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:33,958
--abort goes back to sequencer/head, so
picks that had succeeded are undone as well,

52
00:03:33,998 --> 00:03:36,144
and resolution work is lost.

53
00:03:36,484 --> 00:03:40,558
Conflicts. In one sentence: in a cherry-pick
conflict, "ours" is your branch,

54
00:03:40,598 --> 00:03:45,047
"theirs" is the picked commit, and the base
is the picked commit's parent: a version

55
00:03:45,087 --> 00:03:47,932
of the file that your branch
may never have contained.

56
00:03:48,272 --> 00:03:53,794
Two things in that sentence. First, the
sides are not swapped here as they are in a

57
00:03:53,834 --> 00:03:59,223
rebase: HEAD is your branch. Second, the
base. It's not the merge base of the two

58
00:03:59,263 --> 00:04:04,786
branches. It's the parent of the picked
commit, and that parent may already contain

59
00:04:04,826 --> 00:04:08,271
changes from its own branch
that you have never had.

60
00:04:08,311 --> 00:04:13,833
On screen the base is 08f4771. The merge
base, the most recent commit both branches

61
00:04:13,873 --> 00:04:15,040
share, is 93787ec.

62
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:19,701
With the base in view, a conflict reads as
an instruction: the change to carry over

63
00:04:19,741 --> 00:04:23,958
is the difference between base and theirs.
Apply that difference to your version.

64
00:04:24,298 --> 00:04:29,065
Empty picks. In one sentence: when the
picked change is already present on your

65
00:04:29,105 --> 00:04:33,750
branch, the merge result equals HEAD,
there is nothing to commit, and --empty

66
00:04:33,790 --> 00:04:35,332
decides what happens next.

67
00:04:35,672 --> 00:04:40,622
The textbook's version note. Older
behavior: the only control was

68
00:04:40,662 --> 00:04:45,152
--keep-redundant-commits. Current
behavior, since Git 2.45:

69
00:04:45,192 --> 00:04:51,447
--empty=(drop|keep|stop). Recommended:
--empty=drop in scripts that backport lists

70
00:04:51,487 --> 00:04:57,589
of commits, so that a commit which is
already present doesn't halt an unattended

71
00:04:57,629 --> 00:04:57,896
job.

72
00:04:59,736 --> 00:05:02,223
Picture two ways of laying
a new stretch of track.

73
00:05:02,563 --> 00:05:07,971
A rebase lays the whole new stretch next
to the old line, and when the last sleeper

74
00:05:08,011 --> 00:05:12,894
is down, it throws one switch. Until
then trains still run on the old line.

75
00:05:12,934 --> 00:05:16,111
If work stops halfway, the
old line is untouched.

76
00:05:16,451 --> 00:05:21,675
A cherry-pick of a range extends the line
you are standing on, sleeper by sleeper.

77
00:05:21,715 --> 00:05:26,940
If work stops halfway, the line is longer
than it was, and a train can already run

78
00:05:26,980 --> 00:05:28,096
onto the new part.

79
00:05:28,436 --> 00:05:33,220
Where does the picture break? The half-built
rebase track is hard to find, since

80
00:05:33,260 --> 00:05:37,983
only a detached HEAD reaches it, while
the half-extended cherry-pick line is in

81
00:05:38,023 --> 00:05:42,686
plain sight on the branch. That makes
the cherry-pick state easier to see, and

82
00:05:42,726 --> 00:05:47,329
easier to ship by accident. A tag, a
build or a push at that moment takes the

83
00:05:47,369 --> 00:05:50,344
partial result. Twig looks
worried, and fairly so.

84
00:05:52,184 --> 00:05:57,730
Left: the stopped range pick. The branch
label sits on the copy of the first commit,

85
00:05:57,770 --> 00:06:02,185
and HEAD is on the branch. Right:
the stopped rebase from video 53.

86
00:06:02,225 --> 00:06:06,574
HEAD is detached on the copy, and
the branch is still the old tip.

87
00:06:08,414 --> 00:06:10,358
Part 1: a range that stops.

88
00:06:15,498 --> 00:06:20,221
git cherry-pick 🟡 CAUTION. Which commits
are in that range, and in which order will

89
00:06:20,261 --> 00:06:22,747
they be applied? Say it
out loud. I'll wait.

90
00:06:27,587 --> 00:06:32,386
Three commits, oldest first. main~3, "Move
to the v2 generate endpoint", is the

91
00:06:32,426 --> 00:06:35,632
excluded boundary. The first
of the three was copied.

92
00:06:35,672 --> 00:06:40,717
The second conflicts, because it edits a
line that the excluded commit had changed,

93
00:06:40,757 --> 00:06:43,779
and the sequence stopped
there with exit status 1.

94
00:06:44,119 --> 00:06:48,365
Now the engineer's question from the
opening. Quick quiz, two options.

95
00:06:48,405 --> 00:06:52,835
Option one: HEAD is detached and the
branch hasn't moved, as in a rebase.

96
00:06:52,875 --> 00:06:56,508
Option two: HEAD is on the
branch, and the branch has moved.

97
00:06:56,548 --> 00:06:57,488
Say it out loud.

98
00:07:02,328 --> 00:07:07,830
Option two. HEAD -> release/1.4, at
e0c8403: the copy of the first commit.

99
00:07:07,870 --> 00:07:11,874
HEAD is not detached and the
branch has already moved.

100
00:07:11,914 --> 00:07:18,090
CHERRY_PICK_HEAD is ebcee16. The sequencer's
todo holds it and the commit after it,

101
00:07:18,130 --> 00:07:22,659
and head holds 93787ec, where
HEAD was before the first pick.

102
00:07:22,999 --> 00:07:27,039
git status names the commit it is
stuck on and lists three of the exits.

103
00:07:27,379 --> 00:07:31,081
Part 2: the four exits. Each
from the same stopped state.

104
00:07:31,121 --> 00:07:36,531
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds: for
abort, skip, quit and continue, write how

105
00:07:36,571 --> 00:07:39,616
many of the three commits
end up on the branch.

106
00:07:41,956 --> 00:07:47,746
--abort: back at 93787ec. The pick
that had succeeded is undone as well.

107
00:07:47,786 --> 00:07:49,203
None of the three.

108
00:07:49,543 --> 00:07:53,389
--skip: the conflicting commit is
dropped and the rest is applied.

109
00:07:53,429 --> 00:07:57,981
Two commits on the branch instead of
three, and no trace of the one in between

110
00:07:58,021 --> 00:07:59,217
unless someone looks.

111
00:07:59,557 --> 00:08:04,662
--quit only deletes the bookkeeping. The
completed copy stays on the branch, and the

112
00:08:04,702 --> 00:08:07,908
conflict stays in the index
and the working tree: UU.

113
00:08:07,948 --> 00:08:12,195
And --continue afterwards says "no
cherry-pick or revert in progress".

114
00:08:12,235 --> 00:08:17,279
This is the state the two-in-the-morning
script would leave if it had tried to tidy

115
00:08:17,319 --> 00:08:18,687
up with the wrong exit.

116
00:08:19,027 --> 00:08:24,135
--continue, after a real resolution: the
stopped commit is committed with Asha as

117
00:08:24,175 --> 00:08:26,931
author, and the last
one follows. All three.

118
00:08:27,271 --> 00:08:31,683
Part 3: a conflict in detail.
labs/run ch10/pick-conflict.

119
00:08:31,723 --> 00:08:38,053
One commit this time, with -x, and with the
conflict style set to diff3 so that the

120
00:08:38,093 --> 00:08:40,356
markers show the base as well.

121
00:08:47,196 --> 00:08:52,122
Three versions of one line. Yours,
under HEAD, calls /v1/generate.

122
00:08:52,162 --> 00:08:57,917
The picked commit, at the bottom, calls
/v2/generate with a retries argument.

123
00:08:57,957 --> 00:09:04,238
The base in the middle, labelled "parent of
ebcee16", calls /v2/generate without it.

124
00:09:04,578 --> 00:09:09,232
Stop on the base. Your branch has
never contained a line with /v2/.

125
00:09:09,272 --> 00:09:12,104
Where does it come from? Say it out loud.

126
00:09:22,444 --> 00:09:25,617
The merge base of the two
branches has the v1 line.

127
00:09:25,657 --> 00:09:30,467
But stage 1 is the parent of the picked
commit, main~2, which already has v2.

128
00:09:30,507 --> 00:09:34,310
That is the unusual base of a
cherry-pick, visible in a file.

129
00:09:34,650 --> 00:09:38,800
With the base in view, the
conflict reads as an instruction.

130
00:09:38,840 --> 00:09:43,478
The difference between base and
theirs is: add retries=MAX_RETRIES.

131
00:09:43,518 --> 00:09:46,760
Apply that to your line,
which still says /v1/.

132
00:09:51,900 --> 00:09:56,825
Author Asha Rao, committer Lab User, and
the "cherry picked from commit" line in the

133
00:09:56,865 --> 00:10:01,731
message. The diff of the copy changes one
thing, the argument, as the original did.

134
00:10:01,771 --> 00:10:06,519
Taking "theirs" whole would also have
switched the release to the v2 endpoint: an

135
00:10:06,559 --> 00:10:10,657
unrelated change from main, smuggled
in through a conflict resolution.

136
00:10:10,697 --> 00:10:14,262
That shortcut is tempting, and
almost everyone tries it once.

137
00:10:14,602 --> 00:10:20,424
Part 4: how to finish. labs/run
ch10/pick-finish. Git has prepared the commit

138
00:10:20,464 --> 00:10:23,165
message before you resolve anything.

139
00:10:23,505 --> 00:10:29,223
.git/MERGE_MSG holds the subject, the -x
line, and a comment block listing the

140
00:10:29,263 --> 00:10:34,464
conflicts. Three ways to commit.
Predict the message each one produces.

141
00:10:34,504 --> 00:10:35,202
I'll wait.

142
00:10:40,042 --> 00:10:44,000
--continue commits with that message
and removes the comment lines.

143
00:10:44,340 --> 00:10:49,648
git commit --no-edit skips the editor and
with it the cleanup that strips comment

144
00:10:49,688 --> 00:10:53,015
lines, so # Conflicts:
becomes part of the message.

145
00:10:53,355 --> 00:10:57,840
-m replaces the prepared message,
and the line that -x wrote is gone.

146
00:10:57,880 --> 00:11:02,891
The author is preserved in all three,
because CHERRY_PICK_HEAD still existed.

147
00:11:02,931 --> 00:11:05,973
The rule: finish a
cherry-pick with --continue.

148
00:11:06,313 --> 00:11:11,662
One disagreement between documentation
and behavior, which the textbook records.

149
00:11:11,702 --> 00:11:17,118
The local manual says of -x that the line
is added "only for cherry picks without

150
00:11:17,158 --> 00:11:22,709
conflicts". The transcripts show Git 2.55.0
writing it into the prepared message of

151
00:11:22,749 --> 00:11:26,683
a conflicted pick, and --continue
keeping it. The run wins.

152
00:11:27,023 --> 00:11:34,323
Part 5: empty picks. labs/run ch10/pick-empty.
The fix is already on release/1.4.

153
00:11:34,363 --> 00:11:37,132
Someone picks it a second time.

154
00:11:37,472 --> 00:11:40,993
"The previous cherry-pick is
now empty", exit status 1.

155
00:11:41,033 --> 00:11:45,136
The default is to stop and ask.
git cherry-pick --skip moves on.

156
00:11:45,176 --> 00:11:50,121
The suggested git commit --allow-empty
would record a commit without changes.

157
00:11:50,461 --> 00:11:52,306
The decision can be made in advance.

158
00:11:52,646 --> 00:11:58,454
--empty=drop: one line of notice, "patch
contents already upstream", and no new

159
00:11:58,494 --> 00:11:58,972
commit.

160
00:11:59,312 --> 00:12:03,059
--empty=keep: a commit with no stat lines.

161
00:12:03,399 --> 00:12:06,508
The old name still works
and gives the same result.

162
00:12:06,548 --> 00:12:11,385
--allow-empty, by the way, is a different
option: it concerns commits that were

163
00:12:11,425 --> 00:12:12,620
empty to begin with.

164
00:12:14,460 --> 00:12:16,108
Five mistakes to watch for.

165
00:12:16,448 --> 00:12:19,930
Assuming the branch is untouched
while a range pick is stopped.

166
00:12:19,970 --> 00:12:24,178
Root cause: unlike a rebase, a cherry-pick
commits on the branch as it goes;

167
00:12:24,218 --> 00:12:27,196
finished picks are already
on it and HEAD is attached.

168
00:12:27,536 --> 00:12:32,289
Using --quit to get out of a stopped
pick. Root cause: it removes only the

169
00:12:32,329 --> 00:12:37,147
bookkeeping; the completed copies stay
and the conflict stays in the index.

170
00:12:37,487 --> 00:12:40,380
Resolving a pick conflict
by taking "theirs" whole.

171
00:12:40,420 --> 00:12:45,095
Root cause: theirs is the full file from
the other line, which may carry unrelated

172
00:12:45,135 --> 00:12:48,085
changes that the picked
commit's parent already had.

173
00:12:48,425 --> 00:12:54,056
Finishing a pick with git commit -m. Root
cause: -m replaces the prepared message,

174
00:12:54,096 --> 00:12:56,338
including the line that -x wrote.

175
00:12:56,678 --> 00:13:01,084
Scripts that cherry-pick lists of commits
without checking the exit status.

176
00:13:01,124 --> 00:13:05,885
Root cause: a conflict or an empty pick
stops the sequence with a non-zero status

177
00:13:05,925 --> 00:13:07,604
and a partly advanced branch.

178
00:13:09,444 --> 00:13:13,366
Now, out of the lab, and back
to the script from the opening.

179
00:13:13,406 --> 00:13:18,563
The textbook's guidance is two sentences:
a sequence that stops in a CI job or a

180
00:13:18,603 --> 00:13:22,135
release script leaves a branch
that is partly advanced.

181
00:13:22,175 --> 00:13:27,526
A script that cherry-picks must check the
exit status and decide: --abort to return

182
00:13:27,566 --> 00:13:29,995
to a known state, never a blind retry.

183
00:13:30,335 --> 00:13:35,660
For the release tooling of a model-serving
team, that becomes three lines of policy.

184
00:13:35,700 --> 00:13:40,834
One: every git cherry-pick in a script is
followed by a check of its exit status.

185
00:13:40,874 --> 00:13:45,880
Two: on failure the script runs git
cherry-pick --abort and stops, and a person

186
00:13:45,920 --> 00:13:50,160
takes over with git status, which
names the commit it was stuck on.

187
00:13:50,200 --> 00:13:55,397
Three: lists of backports are run with
--empty=drop, so that a fix that is already

188
00:13:55,437 --> 00:13:57,952
present does not halt an unattended job.

189
00:13:58,292 --> 00:14:02,963
And for the person who takes over a
half-finished backport by hand: read

190
00:14:03,003 --> 00:14:08,460
CHERRY_PICK_HEAD, read .git/sequencer/todo
and head, and write down where the branch

191
00:14:08,500 --> 00:14:12,190
was before the first pick.
Then choose an exit knowingly.

192
00:14:14,030 --> 00:14:18,338
Your turn. Do Lab 10.2, "A
cherry-pick conflict", in

193
00:14:18,378 --> 00:14:21,432
lab-manual/m10-cherry-pick-ranges.md.

194
00:14:21,772 --> 00:14:25,017
When the pick stops, before
you edit the file, write down:

195
00:14:25,357 --> 00:14:30,898
Stage 1, stage 2 and stage 3 of the
conflicted line, and for each, the commit it

196
00:14:30,938 --> 00:14:34,944
comes from. Is stage 1 the
merge base of the two branches?

197
00:14:35,284 --> 00:14:38,685
The difference between stage
1 and stage 3 in one phrase.

198
00:14:38,725 --> 00:14:41,221
That is the change you have to carry over.

199
00:14:41,561 --> 00:14:45,889
What the file would contain if you took
stage 3 whole, and what unrelated change

200
00:14:45,929 --> 00:14:46,817
that would bring.

201
00:14:47,157 --> 00:14:49,584
Then resolve, and finish
with the right command.

202
00:14:49,924 --> 00:14:56,067
The challenge is Exercise 10.9, Level 4,
"a backport that stopped at its second

203
00:14:56,107 --> 00:14:59,588
commit", in
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

204
00:15:01,428 --> 00:15:06,347
Q153: "Compare the state after a stopped
cherry-pick of a range with the state after

205
00:15:06,387 --> 00:15:10,480
a stopped rebase. Where is HEAD, and
what has happened to the branch?"

206
00:15:12,820 --> 00:15:17,562
Answer out loud first. A strong answer is
a comparison in parallel: for each of the

207
00:15:17,602 --> 00:15:21,998
two, where HEAD is, where the branch ref
is, which ref names the commit being

208
00:15:22,038 --> 00:15:26,723
applied, and where the rest of the plan is
stored. It then explains the difference

209
00:15:26,763 --> 00:15:31,448
from what each operation is building, so
that it's a consequence and not a fact to

210
00:15:31,488 --> 00:15:31,966
memorize.

211
00:15:32,306 --> 00:15:36,470
It ends with what the difference means
for the exits and for automation: what

212
00:15:36,510 --> 00:15:40,565
"abort" has to undo in each case, and
what a script risks if it carries on.

213
00:15:42,405 --> 00:15:46,532
Let's land this. You should now be
able to say, in your own words:

214
00:15:46,872 --> 00:15:51,808
A stopped cherry-pick has CHERRY_PICK_HEAD
for the commit being applied and, for a

215
00:15:51,848 --> 00:15:56,177
range, .git/sequencer/ with the
remaining picks and the starting commit.

216
00:15:56,217 --> 00:16:00,364
HEAD stays on the branch, and picks
that already succeeded are on it.

217
00:16:00,704 --> 00:16:06,919
--continue commits and goes on, --skip leaves
the commit out, --abort returns to the

218
00:16:06,959 --> 00:16:12,877
state before the first pick, and --quit
leaves a partly advanced branch with the

219
00:16:12,917 --> 00:16:14,217
conflict in place.

220
00:16:14,557 --> 00:16:19,685
In a conflict, ours is my branch, theirs
is the picked commit, and the base is the

221
00:16:19,725 --> 00:16:23,781
picked commit's parent, which may
show lines my branch never had.

222
00:16:23,821 --> 00:16:29,074
A pick whose change is already present
becomes empty, and --empty decides whether to

223
00:16:29,114 --> 00:16:30,460
stop, drop or keep it.

224
00:16:32,300 --> 00:16:35,842
Read sections 10.6 to 10.8 of Chapter 10.

225
00:16:36,182 --> 00:16:42,489
Do Exercise 10.7, Level 3, ""The
previous cherry-pick is now empty"", in

226
00:16:42,529 --> 00:16:45,399
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

227
00:16:45,739 --> 00:16:50,724
Today you read a stopped cherry-pick from
its files, and you know what each exit

228
00:16:50,764 --> 00:16:55,938
leaves on the branch. Do Lab 10.2 before
the next video, and write the three stages

229
00:16:55,978 --> 00:17:00,838
down before you edit. Next time: the
backport workflow, duplicate commits, and

230
00:17:00,878 --> 00:17:05,550
revert as the inverse. Until then, look
at the state first and type second.

231
00:17:05,590 --> 00:17:07,057
See you in the next one.
